THE GOAL
Each title should share as many words as possible with the core of the target role, while staying true to what the candidate did there. The best result is an exact copy of the core of the target role. That is rarely possible, so the usual result is the target role and the candidate's own role joined together. The recruiter should recognise the title at a glance and never have to wonder whether the candidate fits. The candidate must not lie.

STEP 1 - THE CORE OF THE TARGET ROLE
The posting headline is long. Reduce it to the two or three words a recruiter actually searches for: the name of the role itself. Drop seniority (Senior, Staff, Lead, Principal), programming languages, tools and technologies, industries, products, team names and locations. A word after a comma or a dash that names a kind of the same craft (Backend, Frontend, Platform, Data, Mobile) joins the role and gives a specific variant, while the plain role name stays as the broad variant. Words after a comma or a dash that name a team, a product, a customer or an industry are dropped. Examples: "Golang Backend Engineer, Financial Product Fraud" gives the specific variant "Backend Engineer". "Software Engineer, Backend" gives the specific variant "Backend Engineer" and the broad variant "Software Engineer". "Software Engineer, Platform" gives "Platform Engineer" and "Software Engineer". "Staff Data Engineer, Growth" gives "Data Engineer". Use the specific variant for a title when step 2 shows the candidate really did that work there, otherwise use the broad variant.

STEP 2 - WHICH ROLES THE CANDIDATE REALLY HELD
For each title, read its work text and decide which roles the candidate really performed in that place.
- A role counts when the work text shows the candidate doing the core work of that role as a real part of the job. It does not have to be the main part.
- A role also counts when it is a broader name of the candidate's own role: every Frontend Developer is a Software Developer, every Tax Accountant is an Accountant.
- A role does not count when the work text shows only something nearby, or when it is a different profession: a manager, lead or head of people, a scientist or researcher, an analyst, a designer, a consultant, a salesperson, a product or project manager.
- A technology, language, tool or industry is never a role.

STEP 3 - BUILD THE TITLE
Start from the core of the target role and the candidate's own title.
a) The candidate's own title already is the target role in its own words (for example the title "AI Engineer" and the target "AI Engineer"). Engineer and Developer count as the same noun. Use the wording of the target exactly and drop extra words that are not needed, such as a second specialization or a second role. This case applies only to an exact match of the candidate's own role. If the target role is only a broader name or a secondary role of the candidate, use case b and never case a. Keep a word only if it carries a different identity, such as Founder, and put the target first. Examples: "Data Analyst / BI" for target "Data Analyst" becomes "Data Analyst". "Founder and Data Analyst" becomes "Data Analyst & Founder".
b) The candidate really held the target role (step 2), as a secondary role or as a broader name. Put the target role first, then the candidate's own role, joined with " & " or "/". Use the noun of the target. Examples: own "Backend Developer", target "DevOps Engineer", work text shows building deployment pipelines: "DevOps Engineer & Backend Developer". Own "Data Analyst", target "Software Engineer", work text shows writing production code: "Software Engineer / Data Analyst".
c) The target role is not one the candidate held, but the work text shows part of its function. Keep the title and add the function in parentheses, in plain words taken from the work text, never using the role noun itself. Example: target "Product Manager", work text says "wrote the roadmap and ran customer interviews": "Founder & Backend Developer (roadmap and customer research)".
d) Nothing fits. Keep the title unchanged.
The candidate's own main specialization, the first role words of the title (for example the AI in AI Engineer), stays in every new title, together with identity words such as Founder. Only secondary extra words may be dropped, and only when they are of no use for the target. Never remove the main specialization to make room for the target.

LIMITS
- Never add or raise seniority, and never remove a level the candidate has.
- The name of a technology, language, tool, industry or domain may appear in a title only if the work text names that same thing.
- Never write a role the step 2 rules do not allow, and never invent a role from the posting.
- The golden test for every change: would the candidate's former employer confirm the new wording as an accurate description of the job? If there is any doubt, keep the title.

FORMS
- kind="main" is the headline under the candidate's name. Keep the name, the separators and the punctuation, and change only the role words.
- If the title contains the company name or other words around the role, keep them exactly and change only the role words. Never change company names or dates.
- Follow the candidate's note when there is one.

OUTPUT RULES
- Output exactly one tag for every title above, using the same id.
- status="changed" with the new wording as the content, or status="same" with the current wording as the content.
- reason, at most 20 words. For changed, say which role the title now shows and what supports it: "exact target", or the role that was added with a short phrase from the work text in single quotes. For same, say why nothing changed in a few words.
- Never use a double quote or the characters < and > inside a reason. Use single quotes for quotations. Plain text only.

OUTPUT FORMAT
<title_suggestion id="0" status="changed" reason="{exact target, or the added role with a quote from the work text}">{new wording}</title_suggestion>
<title_suggestion id="1" status="same" reason="{why unchanged}">{current wording}</title_suggestion>